WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Averages that learn.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:11.467
Roll a fair die. Each roll is random, but the average of many rolls heads to three and a half: the expected value.

3
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:20.900
Grey dots are single rolls. The blue line is the running average. Early on it swings wildly; with more rolls the noise cancels out.

4
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:27.267
That's the law of large numbers, and it's why an agent can learn from noisy experience.

5
00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:35.767
You don't need to store every roll to keep the average. After each new value r, nudge the old estimate.

6
00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:42.133
New estimate equals old estimate, plus a step size, times the error: how surprised we were.

7
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:52.367
Say the average was 3.2 after four rolls and the fifth roll is a six. The estimate moves a fifth of the way up, to 3.76.

8
00:00:52.367 --> 00:01:01.033
Remember this shape. In week three it picks the best quiz style, and Q-learning uses it with a fixed step size alpha.

